WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:05.783
Imagine a Torrance workshop on extreme heat, flooding, and industrial land.

2
00:00:05.783 --> 00:00:10.283
The regional map shows several hazards, but the room hears different stories.

3
00:00:10.283 --> 00:00:13.673
A renter describes an apartment that holds heat overnight.

4
00:00:13.673 --> 00:00:17.179
A worker waits for a bus beside heavy traffic with no shade.

5
00:00:17.179 --> 00:00:19.751
A business owner worries about flood access.

6
00:00:19.751 --> 00:00:28.751
A planner can map hazards, but environmental justice asks who is exposed, who is sensitive, who has resources to adapt, and who helps choose the response.

7
00:00:28.751 --> 00:00:35.151
This episode is preparation, not legal advice about a general plan, environmental document, or equity finding.

8
00:00:35.151 --> 00:00:40.503
Environmental-justice duties, climate projections, funding rules, and regional tools change.

9
00:00:40.503 --> 00:00:47.485
Use current statutes, adopted plans, authoritative data, community evidence, and advice from the Torrance city attorney.

10
00:00:47.485 --> 00:00:52.547
Environmental justice joins environmental conditions with fairness in public decisions.

11
00:00:52.547 --> 00:00:57.434
It examines the distribution of pollution, hazards, benefits, and public investment.

12
00:00:57.434 --> 00:01:05.637
It also examines procedure: who receives notice, who can participate, whose knowledge counts, and whether participation changes the decision.

13
00:01:05.637 --> 00:01:16.459
California general-plan law requires qualifying jurisdictions to address environmental justice in the general plan, either in a separate element or through integrated goals and policies.

14
00:01:16.459 --> 00:01:30.771
Required subjects include pollution exposure and other issues specified by current law, along with objectives and policies that reduce health risks, promote civic engagement, and prioritize improvements and programs for disadvantaged communities.

15
00:01:30.771 --> 00:01:35.019
The exact duty must be read from current statute and local circumstances.

16
00:01:35.019 --> 00:01:39.266
Do not treat environmental justice as a label placed on one neighborhood.

17
00:01:39.266 --> 00:01:42.407
Conditions vary block by block and across populations.

18
00:01:42.407 --> 00:01:47.644
A community can face high pollution exposure without fitting one screening-tool threshold.

19
00:01:47.644 --> 00:01:52.589
A regional percentile is a comparison, not a complete description of a person's life.

20
00:01:52.589 --> 00:01:54.177
Use a four-part baseline.

21
00:01:54.177 --> 00:02:04.147
First, map burdens: air pollution, traffic exposure, contaminated sites, noise, heat, flood, wildfire smoke, water stress, or other locally relevant hazards.

22
00:02:04.147 --> 00:02:15.323
Second, map people and sensitivity: age, disability, health, housing condition, outdoor work, language, income, vehicle access, and other factors supported by appropriate data.

23
00:02:15.323 --> 00:02:24.976
Third, map adaptive capacity: shade, cooling, insurance, savings, transit, health care, social networks, emergency communication, and public facilities.

24
00:02:24.976 --> 00:02:28.215
Fourth, map public investment and decision history.

25
00:02:28.215 --> 00:02:32.342
The fourth layer matters because resilience is not only physical.

26
00:02:32.342 --> 00:02:39.328
A neighborhood that repeatedly receives plans without funded projects may distrust another engagement process.

27
00:02:39.328 --> 00:02:45.488
A community with strong local organizations may recover faster even when income measures are low.

28
00:02:45.488 --> 00:02:49.361
Qualitative evidence can explain what a numeric index misses.

29
00:02:49.361 --> 00:03:03.142
SCAG's April 2023 public Toolbox Tuesday on integrating equity and adaptation into planning emphasized bringing those lenses into the planning and environmental process rather than adding them after project selection.

30
00:03:03.142 --> 00:03:09.683
Its 2024 Environmental Justice Tools for Local Planning session presented resources for local analysis.

31
00:03:09.683 --> 00:03:14.572
Its 2024 heat-proofing training addressed resilience planning and mitigation.

32
00:03:14.572 --> 00:03:18.700
These materials support a workflow, not a single mandatory score.

33
00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:22.955
Regional resilience asks how connected systems behave under stress.

34
00:03:22.955 --> 00:03:25.813
Electricity powers cooling and communication.

35
00:03:25.813 --> 00:03:30.067
Transportation provides evacuation, work access, and medical trips.

36
00:03:30.067 --> 00:03:33.242
Water systems depend on energy and infrastructure.

37
00:03:33.242 --> 00:03:36.291
Housing condition affects indoor heat and smoke.

38
00:03:36.291 --> 00:03:39.720
A failure in one system can cascade into another city.

39
00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:46.896
SCAG's 2025 water-resilience training and 2026 Regional Resilience Toolkit session add this regional perspective.

40
00:03:46.896 --> 00:03:52.421
Hazards cross boundaries, watersheds, utility territories, and transportation networks.

41
00:03:52.421 --> 00:03:59.406
Torrance cannot plan port access, air quality, water reliability, and evacuation as if city limits were walls.

42
00:03:59.406 --> 00:04:01.578
Resilience has at least four stages.

43
00:04:01.578 --> 00:04:03.992
Prepare by reducing known vulnerability.

44
00:04:03.992 --> 00:04:07.975
Withstand by keeping critical functions operating during an event.

45
00:04:07.975 --> 00:04:12.078
Recover by restoring services and housing without displacing people.

46
00:04:12.078 --> 00:04:16.966
Adapt by changing investments and standards as conditions and projections change.

47
00:04:16.966 --> 00:04:19.138
Test every strategy across all four.

48
00:04:19.138 --> 00:04:26.380
A cooling center helps during heat but fails if residents cannot reach it, do not know it exists, or fear leaving a pet.

49
00:04:26.380 --> 00:04:31.871
Tree planting can reduce heat but requires water, maintenance, species selection, and time.

50
00:04:31.871 --> 00:04:35.491
Home retrofits may help owners while renters remain exposed.

51
00:04:35.491 --> 00:04:39.353
Flood protection can shift water or raise nearby property costs.

52
00:04:39.353 --> 00:04:42.129
A good strategy anticipates secondary effects.

53
00:04:42.129 --> 00:04:45.931
Use future climate conditions where the decision requires them.

54
00:04:45.931 --> 00:04:53.293
Historic averages alone can understate heat, precipitation, sea-level, or fire-smoke risk over the life of infrastructure.

55
00:04:53.293 --> 00:04:56.913
State and regional tools provide scenarios, not certainties.

56
00:04:56.913 --> 00:05:00.715
Record the scenario, time horizon, resolution, and uncertainty.

57
00:05:00.715 --> 00:05:07.413
For practical application, conduct an equity-and-resilience stress test for a fictional Torrance corridor plan.

58
00:05:07.413 --> 00:05:09.827
Choose one hazard, such as extreme heat.

59
00:05:09.827 --> 00:05:13.689
Identify exposure, sensitive populations, and adaptive capacity.

60
00:05:13.689 --> 00:05:16.766
Trace two systems, such as transit and electricity.

61
00:05:16.766 --> 00:05:21.534
Describe what happens during a three-day event, including nighttime conditions.

62
00:05:21.534 --> 00:05:23.465
Then evaluate three investments.

63
00:05:23.465 --> 00:05:26.844
One can be site-scale, such as shade and cool materials.

64
00:05:26.844 --> 00:05:31.068
One can be neighborhood-scale, such as a resilient community facility.

65
00:05:31.068 --> 00:05:35.051
One can be regional, such as coordinated transit or grid planning.

66
00:05:35.051 --> 00:05:43.921
For each, identify beneficiaries, burdens, responsible agency, funding source, maintenance, performance measure, and potential displacement effect.

67
00:05:43.921 --> 00:05:46.713
Engagement should be designed as infrastructure.

68
00:05:46.713 --> 00:05:53.753
Budget for translation, interpretation, childcare, accessible formats, trusted messengers, and compensation where lawful.

69
00:05:53.753 --> 00:05:55.847
Offer more than one time and method.

70
00:05:55.847 --> 00:06:00.908
Return to participants with a decision log showing what changed, what did not, and why.

71
00:06:00.908 --> 00:06:04.282
Avoid extracting stories to decorate a predetermined plan.

72
00:06:04.282 --> 00:06:11.613
Ask residents what conditions they observe, what coping strategies they use, and which interventions would create new burdens.

73
00:06:11.613 --> 00:06:15.976
Combine that knowledge with health, environmental, and infrastructure data.

74
00:06:15.976 --> 00:06:19.350
Protect privacy when the population or geography is small.

75
00:06:19.350 --> 00:06:21.619
Prioritization needs explicit criteria.

76
00:06:21.619 --> 00:06:33.254
Possible criteria include severity, number of people exposed, vulnerability, community priority, readiness, co-benefits, cost, maintenance capacity, and whether the investment corrects a historic gap.

77
00:06:33.254 --> 00:06:34.417
Publish the weights.

78
00:06:34.417 --> 00:06:38.199
Test whether a different reasonable weighting changes the result.

79
00:06:38.199 --> 00:06:41.573
Regional coordination does not erase local accountability.

80
00:06:41.573 --> 00:06:49.601
Name the action Torrance controls, the action requiring a neighboring jurisdiction or agency, and the forum where coordination will occur.

81
00:06:49.601 --> 00:06:55.942
Add a trigger for revisiting the plan after a new climate projection, major event, or infrastructure failure.

82
00:06:55.942 --> 00:06:58.211
Measure both distribution and outcomes.

83
00:06:58.211 --> 00:07:08.916
Count where money goes, but also track indoor temperature, shade coverage, service continuity, travel time to relief, exposure reduction, housing stability, and participant assessment.

84
00:07:08.916 --> 00:07:13.628
Disaggregate carefully enough to reveal differences without exposing individuals.

85
00:07:13.628 --> 00:07:16.653
Use a scenario register to keep assumptions visible.

86
00:07:16.653 --> 00:07:24.739
For each hazard, record the projection source, planning horizon, severity case, affected assets, exposed populations, and confidence level.

87
00:07:24.739 --> 00:07:30.441
Add an early-action threshold, such as repeated service interruption or a revised heat projection.

88
00:07:30.441 --> 00:07:34.455
Then name the person or agency responsible for reviewing the trigger.

89
00:07:34.455 --> 00:07:38.469
This turns adaptation from a distant aspiration into a managed cycle.

90
00:07:38.469 --> 00:07:42.890
It also prevents a commission from treating one forecast as permanent truth.

91
00:07:42.890 --> 00:07:53.129
When evidence changes, update the scenario, explain the change publicly, and test whether the investment sequence still protects the people facing the greatest combined burden.

92
00:07:53.129 --> 00:07:54.002
Details change.

93
00:07:54.002 --> 00:08:02.728
Screening tools and resilience toolkits inform judgment, but they do not replace current legal requirements, local knowledge, or city-attorney advice.

94
00:08:02.728 --> 00:08:04.396
Now three recall questions.

95
00:08:04.396 --> 00:08:05.199
Question one.

96
00:08:05.199 --> 00:08:08.968
What four layers belong in an environmental-justice baseline?

97
00:08:08.968 --> 00:08:09.400
Answer.

98
00:08:09.400 --> 00:08:15.764
Burdens, people and sensitivity, adaptive capacity, and the history of public investment and decisions.

99
00:08:15.764 --> 00:08:16.567
Question two.

100
00:08:16.567 --> 00:08:20.212
What are the four stages of resilience used in this lesson?

101
00:08:20.212 --> 00:08:20.645
Answer.

102
00:08:20.645 --> 00:08:23.054
Prepare, withstand, recover, and adapt.

103
00:08:23.054 --> 00:08:23.981
Question three.

104
00:08:23.981 --> 00:08:26.638
Why should a city analyze regional systems?

105
00:08:26.638 --> 00:08:27.070
Answer.

106
00:08:27.070 --> 00:08:37.202
Hazards and infrastructure networks cross city boundaries, and failure in energy, water, transportation, housing, or communication can cascade across jurisdictions.

107
00:08:37.202 --> 00:08:41.095
Your learner artifact is the equity-and-resilience stress test.

108
00:08:41.095 --> 00:08:50.053
Include one hazard profile, two connected systems, three investments at different scales, an engagement plan, and a public decision log template.

109
00:08:50.053 --> 00:08:52.462
Cite public data and label uncertainty.

110
00:08:52.462 --> 00:08:56.540
Do not include personally identifiable or proprietary information.

111
00:08:56.540 --> 00:09:20.557
This lesson draws from SCAG's public 2023 through 2026 Toolbox Tuesday materials, including Integrating Equity and Adaptation into the Planning and Environmental Process, Environmental Justice Tools for Local Planning, Heat-Proofing Communities with Resilience Planning and Mitigation, Water Resilience Challenges and Opportunities, and the Regional Resilience Toolkit.

112
00:09:20.557 --> 00:09:28.628
It also references current California general-plan environmental-justice requirements and public climate-planning resources.

113
00:09:28.628 --> 00:09:30.385
This is original narration.

114
00:09:30.385 --> 00:09:35.137
It copies no source prose, slides, maps, recordings, or proprietary data.

115
00:09:35.137 --> 00:09:41.190
Confirm current law, datasets, projections, adopted plans, and Torrance city-attorney advice.
